## What I changed

A single edit to one method — `addOwner` in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:

```java
Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
boolean telephoneTaken = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
    .anyMatch(existing -> existing.getTelephone().equals(owner.getTelephone()));
if (telephoneTaken) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
}
this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
```

## Why this keeps structural impact low

- **One file, one method.** The file-count multiplier and the per-function cost are both minimized by not introducing new classes, no new `@ExceptionHandler`, no constructor/dependency changes, and no touching the large `ClinicServiceImpl` or `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.
- **No second changed function.** I inlined the check rather than extracting a helper into the controller — a helper would pay the full `WMC_other` of that large class a second time.
- **Reused existing machinery.** Telephone *normalization* already happens upstream: `OwnerFieldsValidator` (registered via `@InitBinder`) strips non-digits to a canonical 10-digit form during request binding, and every owner is persisted through that same path. So comparing the incoming normalized value against stored values via equality is sufficient — no normalization logic needed here.
- The duplicate case returns `409 CONFLICT` directly, avoiding the catch-all `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` in the advice (which would have turned any thrown exception into a 500).

Both new acceptance tests (`coreRejectsDuplicateTelephone`, `functionalityAllowsDistinctTelephone`) and all existing tests pass.
